There are five ways in which Bob's selection could be:

\begin{tabular}[t]{|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \cline{4-6}
$b$ & $b$ & $b$ & $b$ & $b$ & \\ \cline{4-6}
$b$ & $b$ & $b$ & $r$ & $r$ & \\
$b$ & $b$ & $r$ & $b$ & $b$ & \\
$b$ & $b$ & $r$ & $b$ & $r$ & \\ \cline{4-6}
$b$ & $r$ & $b$ & $b$ & $b$ & \\
$b$ & $r$ & $b$ & $b$ & $r$ & \\
\cline{4-6}
\end{tabular}

Out of the first five cases, one of them has the same contents for the bags, and thus 1/5 of these cases work. There are 12 cases that don't, and the probability for that is 11/12. The probability of Bob's selection being the same color is thus (1/5 + 11/12) = \boxed{\frac{41}{60}}. nakne
