In triangle $JNO,$ the sum of the angles must be $180$ degrees because it's an inscribed angle of the circle.

$m\angle JNO+m\angle HNO+m\angle J = 180^{\circ}$

If we subtract $m\angle J$ from both sides, we get $m\angle JNO + m\angle HNO = 180^{\circ} - m\angle J$.

We can do the same thing with triangle $JNH.$  In this case, we get $m\angle JNO + m\angle HNO = m\angle J + 180^{\circ} - m\angle H$.

But, we are trying to find the sum of $m\angle J$ and $m\angle H$ so if we subtract $m\angle JNO + m\angle HNO$ from both sides, we just get $\boxed{180^{\circ}}.$ ⋙. ⋙
By applying the Triangle Angle Sum Theorem to the triangles $\triangle JNO$ and $\triangle JNH,$ we see that $m\angle JNO + m\angle HNO = 180^{\circ} - m\angle J$ and $m\angle JNO + m\angle HNO = m\angle J + 180^{\circ} - m\angle H,$ respectively.  Therefore, $180^{\circ} - m\angle J + m\angle J + 180^{\circ} - m\angle H = 360^{\circ}.$  Subtracting $180^{\circ}$ from both sides then gives $360^{\circ} - 360^{\circ} = 0,$ which is not possible, so we must have $m\angle JNO + m\angle HNO = 0,$ or in other words, $\boxed{m\angle J + m\angle H = 180^{\circ}}.$ ⋙. ⋙
